#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189092} :activity/id #uuid "d3d03d98-f19e-5bee-b9c4-edb0598606c2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048944 :topic/id #uuid "cdc1c937-d8fa-5ff7-934a-a282eda47b10" :topic/math-academy-id 3855} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188283 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187987 :question/id #uuid "2f27b473-e10a-5e77-8bb2-d8ae5d0ecf4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10037"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4e1b67a-cd41-5edc-90b9-941007bfc39c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050921}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502134}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"} :step/id #uuid "83fbb490-e605-5aaf-8930-059c037e6897" :step/math-academy-id 37866 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189157}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189092 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189369 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a normal population with unknown population mean $\\mu$ and *known* population variance $\\sigma^2.$ Recall that a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\overline{x} - z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}, \\: \\overline{x} + z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ is the sample mean, $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nOne thing to realize is that this situation is unrealistic: it's doubtful that we'll not know the population mean yet know the population variance.\n\nA more realistic situation is that $\\sigma^2$ is *unknown*, and we must estimate it using the sample data. To estimate the population variance $\\sigma^2,$ we use the sample variance $S^2,$ given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2.\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n} \\sim T_{n-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $T_{n-1}$ is a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nLet's take a look at a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "98d4374a-d448-5d6a-852b-d1d260b9c6bd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6241 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "68294170-b618-5346-a481-90c4873ac94a" :step/math-academy-id 37870 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189158}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189370 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population with the unknown population mean $\\mu$ and sample variance $s^2.$ Then, for a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\overline{x} - t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}, \\: \\overline{x} +t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T > t_{n-1, \\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ and $T \\sim T_{n-1}.$ \n\nThe endpoints of our confidence interval are called **confidence limits** and are given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm t_{n-1, \\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nEach part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\overline{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean\n\n- $\\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the standard error of the mean\n\n- $t_{n-1,\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $t$-score\n\n- $E = t_{n-1, \\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the margin of error\n\n![](images/10/10cd4a425a9668611335a7e80561944d147faaa9152f91fb9ed87378e8c092eb.svg)\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\overset{―}{x}-[\\text{margin of error}], & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+[\\text{margin of error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{―}{x}-[\\text{[math]t[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}], & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+[\\text{[math]t[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{―}{x}-{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}, & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the expression for the confidence interval is very similar to when the population variance was known. The only difference is that we now use the $t$-scores instead of $z$-scores and the sample standard deviation $s$ instead of the population standard deviation $\\sigma.$\n\nFinally, since computing a confidence interval amounts to finding the corresponding confidence limits, we will use the two terms interchangeably." :tutorial/id #uuid "b3eaa60c-8a64-508a-a094-245270047e8a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6242 :tutorial/title "Summarizing Confidence Intervals for Normal Populations With Unknown Population Variance"} :step/id #uuid "991795bc-9250-5f2f-96cb-2fb57f170b38" :step/math-academy-id 37871 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189091}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189094 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188284 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187988 :question/id #uuid "c2e2bcbc-ab4b-5a0a-a803-cb98326017ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-10040"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "778f1426-e8a0-597f-84f6-337359a553c8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521545}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"} :step/id #uuid "f1d32a2f-ba24-5d90-9872-8adddcce8f50" :step/math-academy-id 37893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188285 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187989 :question/id #uuid "7446f45b-6d41-5a11-8006-853b38fa5142" :question/math-academy-id "e-10047"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81c8b2bc-d215-5bae-b81f-05870dd39163" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049014}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502135}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"} :step/id #uuid "c148a2a5-30ac-549d-a5fc-b8016b9a5d06" :step/math-academy-id 37914 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189094}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189394 :tutorial/content "Up to now, we've assumed that the underlying population is normally distributed. However, if we have a *sufficiently large* sample, then according to the central limit theorem, we have the approximation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\approx N(0,1).\n$$\n\nMoreover, since $n$ is sufficiently large, $\\sigma^2$ can be approximated as $S^2.$ Therefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n} \\approx T_{n-1}.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** If the population is *not* normally distributed, then the sample size $n$ must be sufficiently large for this approximation to apply. Typically, $n\\geq 30$ is sufficient.\n\nIn such situations, we can use our formula for the confidence limits of the population mean $\\mu\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cad33eaf-4573-5886-837e-5a914b347274" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6409 :tutorial/title "Large Samples"} :step/id #uuid "1c95af0f-9fb6-5e0c-89fc-852ce2b16b56" :step/math-academy-id 38729 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189096}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189158 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189395 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=9$ drawn from a normal population. The sample mean is $\\overline{x}=30,$ and the sample standard deviation is $s=3.3.$ \nWe wish to compute a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nRecall that a confidence interval is an interval of the form \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}-E, \\: \\overline{x}+E),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overline{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean, and\n\n- $E$ is the margin of error.\n\nIn our example, the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is also normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\left(\\mu,\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nBy transforming $\\overline{X}$ in the manner described earlier, we have that the random variable $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n},\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution $T_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nWe wish to find a $t$-interval that we're $95\\%$ confident that the random variable $T$ lies within. This interval is indicated in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/63/63973d7f59a993d42b7b8145f3354e9e7d7dba3c0393c756b6a95754eb4ead2e.png)\n\n  \n\nSince we wish to find a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we define our parameter $\\alpha$ as \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ is precisely the area bounded by each \"tail\" that we're excluding from our confidence interval. Let's label the critical values at the endpoints of our interval as $\\pm t_{n-1, \\alpha/2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/b5/b557afdbd649aa159addd9fe01021a7c3561f94b0168fd874876980d8bb7c3f4.png)\n\n  \n\nAccording to our diagram,\n\n$$\nP(T > t_{8,0.025}) = P(T < -t_{8,0.025}) = 0.025.\n$$\n\n  \n\nUsing a table of values for the $t$-distribution, we find that $t_{8,0.025} \\approx 2.306.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(T > 2.306) = P(T < -2.306) = 0.025.\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2.306<T<2.306)=0.95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, there is a $95\\%$ probability that our random variable $T$ will lie in the interval $(-2.306, 2.306).$\n\nSimilar to the case where $\\sigma^2$ was known, we find the appropriate confidence interval by solving the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the population mean $\\mu\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2.306 & <T<2.306 \\\\ -2.306 & <\\frac{\\overset{―}{x}-\\mu }{s/\\sqrt{n}}<2.306 \\\\ -2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} & <\\overset{―}{x}-\\mu <2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ -\\overset{―}{x}-2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} & <-\\mu <-\\overset{―}{x}+2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{x}+2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} & >\\mu >\\overset{―}{x}-2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{x}-2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} & <\\mu <\\overset{―}{x}+2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\overline{x}-2.306 \\cdot\\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}, \\: \\overline{x}+2.306 \\cdot\\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}  \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values\n\n$$\nn=9, \\qquad\n\\overline{x}=30, \\qquad\ns=3.3,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that our $95\\%$ confidence interval for $\\mu$ is \n\n$$\n\\big( 30- 2.537, \\: 30 + 2.537 \\big).\n$$\n\n \n\nDescribing this confidence interval using confidence limits, we have\n\n$$\n30 \\pm 2.537.\n$$\n\nLet's now describe the general procedure." :tutorial/id #uuid "3941a0da-320f-5a13-b99f-f3556ba560ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6410 :tutorial/title "Constructing Confidence Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "a7e1e894-dda6-51e5-9fc8-542ca1fec109" :step/math-academy-id 38730 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189093}}] :activity/title "Confidence Intervals for One Mean: Unknown Population Variance" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186188450}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521594} :activity/id #uuid "34aeab81-66dd-5a0b-8751-468cd63a1098" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048944 :topic/id #uuid "cdc1c937-d8fa-5ff7-934a-a282eda47b10" :topic/math-academy-id 3855} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "256f968b-f149-5a64-b5e7-35483cba2ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.40\\pm 0.131\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520844} {:answer/id #uuid "4d4ed279-f589-55a4-9518-10e65b24f391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.52\\pm 0.133\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520942} {:answer/id #uuid "6561e4f2-6b4f-5178-9f7d-90b9b8551b65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "400\\pm 0.113\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520993} {:answer/id #uuid "76d7e595-409f-5d32-9911-9eac076c3663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.52\\pm 0.311\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521028} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2957a5-69d2-53f1-a6dd-b37bfb959fc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10.92\\pm 0.311\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521193}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521028} :answer-field/id #uuid "31fb2a2b-85f8-5174-8f8c-064217a8d0bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "33652587-e008-57dc-9a3a-905f5c4ae6f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-142975" :question/problem "In a particular state, $400$ randomly selected working adults were asked the distance from their home to their workplace. The sample mean distance was $8.52\\,\\text{mi}$ with standard deviation $2.40\\,\\text{mi}.$ Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the mean distance from home to work  $\\mu $ for all working adults in this state.   \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $398$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |\n| $399$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |\n| $400$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=400\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*.  \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=8.52\\,\\text{mi},\\,n=400,\\,s=2.40\\,\\text{mi}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{399,0.005}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{399,0.005})=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=400-1=399$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/82/829fa68b18545ff59ffa702fe4567997d635089edf4e90d713b1c8d817c7959f.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{399,0.005}=2.588:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $398$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |\n| $399$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $2.588$ |\n| $400$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8.52 & \\pm 2.588\\cdot \\frac{2.40}{\\sqrt{400}}\\,\\text{mi} \\\\ 8.52 & \\pm 0.311\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "10d5d17f-5e05-568d-892a-89daf97c2f8d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "247effcb-626c-5ae3-bd39-1910118941fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "126\\pm 14.048" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520838} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b4e3e8-d9aa-5bcf-bbe3-dae6af3e90a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "99\\pm 8.040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521239} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a7ad11-1129-5b22-9704-503871bfe339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "171\\pm 15.339" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521251} {:answer/id #uuid "dd26c9b8-8eb6-53aa-bf47-535a91ee6396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "252\\pm 16.070" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521259} {:answer/id #uuid "e14ec158-b428-5af8-9f36-458c932a5ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "252\\pm 14.055" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521269}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521259} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce8c2ade-aca3-521a-80e5-387f79992d8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5a9d87e1-81bc-588a-9941-b4e6843c1f57" :question/math-academy-id "q-142514" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=100$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $252,$ and the sample standard deviation is $81,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.984)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $99$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=100\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=252,\\,n=100,\\,s=81.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.984)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{99,0.025}=1.984.\n$$\n\n![](images/5b/5b33840d7c98bbf799bdc58dc90d84752b3fa84c36d4a9b122a20ec9401b6823.png)\n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}252 & \\pm 1.984\\cdot \\frac{81}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\ 252 & \\pm 16.070\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "29756d50-cfb9-5aeb-ab06-3e56c10fad83" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521575}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "066610ff-3d82-50b2-b812-1338bebdf1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\pm 2.576" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520772} {:answer/id #uuid "06c2f12e-68a5-528e-9841-ad944de82b35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60\\pm 1.288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520773} {:answer/id #uuid "09003a28-3717-5e7f-9baa-237b32a73563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60\\pm 7.968" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520778} {:answer/id #uuid "34abe2f4-dc85-5332-b992-cc39f815e618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60\\pm 2.576" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520873} {:answer/id #uuid "84f0eeaf-ad7b-52c3-b682-813bce3f605f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\pm 5.221" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521069}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520778} :answer-field/id #uuid "74d5b3dd-2378-5740-8a14-1eae3756ad16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a4a9f9de-52c8-5bcd-a936-99bb91021b0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-142313" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=64$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $60,$ and the sample standard deviation is $24,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.656)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $63$ degrees of freedom.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=64\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=60,\\,n=64,\\,s=24.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.656)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{63,0.005}=2.656.\n$$\n\n![](images/55/5596a286f89b2eed9fd6340c00e77915f84883f2d57d1b342b762143811537aa.png)\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & \\pm 2.656\\cdot \\frac{24}{\\sqrt{64}} \\\\ 60 & \\pm 7.968\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "43e3e0c5-16b5-54b6-97d1-904aec98a411" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521584}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eb32a81-2dc3-50ed-840b-da7ac0d69d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "174\\pm 1.084\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520794} {:answer/id #uuid "311561ec-4176-5648-a42d-bd951c43964d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\pm 1.356\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520867} {:answer/id #uuid "4d1d33fe-8836-5ceb-9e21-dde0f49573e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "174\\pm 0.542\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520940} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6a617b-52cd-5f5b-a837-7d8fa3497d1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "174\\pm 1.369\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521048} {:answer/id #uuid "f3605f82-ef49-559d-a1ce-7456d966fa96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25\\pm 1.084\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521298}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521048} :answer-field/id #uuid "04f3a275-b656-5d6f-8b7c-3bd7e106dfd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "969c1777-ec8b-5e40-b43c-413b46fc477f" :question/math-academy-id "q-143028" :question/problem "A tire manufacturer measures the stopping distance of a new tire model from $50\\,\\text{mph},$ on a standard test car. A sample of $25$ stopping distances yielded a sample mean of $174\\,\\text{ft}$ with a sample standard deviation of $4\\,\\text{ft}.$ Assuming that the stopping distances are normally distributed, find a $90%$ confidence interval for the mean stopping distance $\\mu $ of these tires.   \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $23$ | $\\,1.319\\,$ | $\\,1.714\\,$ | $\\,2.069\\,$ | $\\,2.500\\,$ | $\\,2.807\\,$ |\n| $24$ | $\\,1.318\\,$ | $\\,1.711\\,$ | $\\,2.064\\,$ | $\\,2.492\\,$ | $\\,2.797\\,$ |\n| $25$ | $\\,1.316\\,$ | $\\,1.708\\,$ | $\\,2.060\\,$ | $\\,2.485\\,$ | $\\,2.787\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=174\\,\\text{ft},\\,n=25,\\,s=4\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{24,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{24,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=25-1=24$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/52/52426e7ca1868359b5ed5c97ba1fabeaebb22b73393918d5b9031c8c6320a300.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{24,0.05}=1.711:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $23$ | $\\,1.319\\,$ | $\\,1.714\\,$ | $\\,2.069\\,$ | $\\,2.500\\,$ | $\\,2.807\\,$ |\n| $24$ | $\\,1.318\\,$ | $1.711$ | $\\,2.064\\,$ | $\\,2.492\\,$ | $\\,2.797\\,$ |\n| $25$ | $\\,1.316\\,$ | $\\,1.708\\,$ | $\\,2.060\\,$ | $\\,2.485\\,$ | $\\,2.787\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}174 & \\pm 1.711\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{25}}\\,\\text{ft} \\\\ 174 & \\pm 1.369\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f75b345d-27d5-5c91-b2b0-43c6291fee8a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d97057b-a0ed-5cd4-a152-34da488534bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14.9\\pm 1.959\\,\\text{g/dl}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520790} {:answer/id #uuid "9a012243-783b-5fcd-a20c-31d094c51f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13.1\\pm 0.959\\,\\text{g/dl}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521113} {:answer/id #uuid "a711e61d-7f5c-5a9e-a2ea-31ecb7333dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\pm 0.995\\,\\text{g/dl}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521138} {:answer/id #uuid "c7a652d0-3e8f-5aee-82bd-bf86b31f4084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.8\\pm 1.995\\,\\text{g/dl}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521207} {:answer/id #uuid "f176a196-bce3-51df-8b5e-2c12622bc68c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13.1\\pm 2.131\\,\\text{g/dl}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521295}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521113} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce4236eb-539a-5a14-a293-5eda94e5a9e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bb725276-ca73-5e90-be79-e741b1537c3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-142976" :question/problem "The amount of hemoglobin in the bloodstream of people living in a particular Colorado community follows a normal distribution. An analysis on a random sample of $16$ people yields a mean hemoglobin concentration of $13.1\\,\\text{g/dl}$ with a sample standard deviation of $1.8\\,\\text{g/dl}.$ Find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean hemoglobin concentration $\\mu $ for all people in the community.  \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $1.341$ | $1.753$ | $2.131$ | $2.602$ | $2.947$ |\n| $16$ | $1.337$ | $1.746$ | $2.120$ | $2.583$ | $2.921$ |\n| $17$ | $1.333$ | $1.740$ | $2.110$ | $2.576$ | $2.898$ |" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=13.1\\,\\text{g/dl},\\,n=16,\\,s=1.8\\,\\text{g/dl}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{15,0.025}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{15,0.025})=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=16-1=15$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/03/03b3b3aec86ef22237d0013b3496993ae4cdda192540fc1c29f01ad608fcb810.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{15,0.025}=2.131:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $1.341$ | $1.753$ | $2.131$ | $2.602$ | $2.947$ |\n| $16$ | $1.337$ | $1.746$ | $2.120$ | $2.583$ | $2.921$ |\n| $17$ | $1.333$ | $1.740$ | $2.110$ | $2.576$ | $2.898$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13.1 & \\pm 2.131\\cdot \\frac{1.8}{\\sqrt{16}}\\,\\text{g/dl} \\\\ 13.1 & \\pm 0.959\\,\\text{g/dl}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fc2ed12d-6e0a-50a2-84a6-8decfa5fc1ae" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521656}}] :activity/title "Confidence Intervals for One Mean: Unknown Population Variance" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520748}]}
